# protected.conf - the set of destination IPs the gate protects.
#
# Copy to /etc/xdpgate/protected.conf and list the addresses to gate.
# This file is the source of truth: xdpgate-load applies it at attach and
# `systemctl reload xdpgate` applies it to a running gate. Anything in the live
# maps that this file does not name is removed on reload.
#
# Traffic to an address listed here is DROPPED unless a valid SPA has opened the
# specific (source IP, proto, dest port) tuple. Traffic to any other address is
# not gated at all.
#
# Format: one address per line. '#' starts a comment; blank lines are ignored.
# CIDR is NOT accepted - the protected maps are exact-match hashes, so give each
# address its own line. Limit is 256 per family.
#
# There is no safe empty default. A gate with nothing in this file passes every
# packet while reporting itself healthy, so xdpgate-load refuses to attach
# rather than come up looking armed and doing nothing.
#
# DO NOT list an address that carries your management plane. The gate is
# stateless and ingress-only, so protecting the source address of your default
# route drops the return path of every connection this host originates - your
# SSH session included. xdpgate-load refuses to arm that specific mistake; run
# `whats-on-ip --preflight` to catch the softer ones (Tailscale underlay).
#
# Migrating a box whose protected set only ever lived in the kernel maps:
#     xdpgate-ctl export > /etc/xdpgate/protected.conf

# A service /128 that shares nothing with SSH or the Tailscale underlay:
2001:db8::1234

# A dedicated IPv4 service address:
203.0.113.50
